{"number":59,"title":"Canon patch (blocked on push scope): executor-routine fixes for #53–#57","body":"The canon changes for #53, #54, #55, #56 and #57 are complete and tested — but **this session cannot push them**. The git proxy returns `403` for `missingbulb/Claudinite`; the session's GitHub scope is `missingbulb/claudinitewebsite` alone. That's an organization egress policy, which the proxy README says to report rather than route around.

So the work is preserved here, to be applied from a session whose environment includes the canon repo.

**Local commit:** `40f316c` — *Stop the executor routine tripping over its own conformance checks* (8 files, +273 / −12)

**Verified before this was written:**
- full canon suite — `1148 pass / 0 fail`
- `node engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs` — 0 blocking (9 advisories, all pre-existing on `main`, unchanged by this diff)
- every new test confirmed to **fail against the unmodified source first**, then pass
- the new `conformance-workflow` rule run against this repo's real `ci.yml`: 1 finding before the fix, 0 after

## To apply

```sh
git checkout -b claude/executor-routine-fixes
git apply canon-executor-fixes.patch
```

## Patch

<details>
<summary><code>git diff main..HEAD</code></summary>

````diff
diff --git a/bootstrap.md b/bootstrap.md
index a7b651d..bd12284 100644
--- a/bootstrap.md
+++ b/bootstrap.md
@@ -217,6 +217,36 @@ node .claudinite/shared/engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs
   whose steps run the project's own tests, if any, then the world command above. The point is a
   deterministic place the whole-repo sweep runs at each change; keep it as small as the repo needs.

+**If that flow is a GitHub Actions workflow, it needs `workflow_dispatch:` — one line, and
+without it the repo's nightly maintenance PR can never land.** Both the branch push and the PR
+open ride the Action's `GITHUB_TOKEN`, and GitHub's recursion guard suppresses workflow runs for
+events that token authors — so a maintenance PR gets **no `pull_request` run at all**, however
+unfiltered the workflow is. `maintenance.delivery: auto-merge` then arms a queue that waits on a
+check which never appears, and baselining stops landing silently. The guard's one documented
+exception is `workflow_dispatch`, which is why baselining's `deliver()` starts the checks itself.
+Give the workflow both triggers, and **no `paths:` filter** (a filtered sweep is a gate that
+happens to be open — the `conformance-workflow` check flags both shapes):
+
+```yaml
+name: CI
+on:
+  pull_request:            # no paths: filter — the sweep must gate every PR
+  push:
+    branches: [main]
+  workflow_dispatch:       # lets baselining start this run on the maintenance PR
+jobs:
+  conformance:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v5
+      - uses: actions/setup-node@v5
+        with:
+          node-version: 22
+      # the project's own tests go here, if it has any
+      - name: Claudinite world-scope conformance sweep
+        run: node .claudinite/shared/engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs
+```
+
 Then run the world sweep once locally and clear what it surfaces. On a repo with existing code,
 **expect a backlog** — enforcement scope is whole-repo, and findings in code you never touched
 would otherwise fail every future run. Fix causes, or record a reasoned `accept` in
